+{"id":"I1","rule_id":"F5-acceptance: managed identity to Entra-only SQL","layer":"persistence","files":["infrastructure/modules/sql.bicep","infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml","infrastructure/pipelines/check-pipeline.py"],"root_cause":"Source: R1 (P1). Layer note: the schema has no infrastructure layer, so every finding here uses the nearest one (SQL and migrations map to persistence, App Service to api). Symptom: the 'Grant the app's managed identity database access' step fails on the first deploy to any environment. Cause: the Microsoft.Sql/servers resource at infrastructure/modules/sql.bicep:11-27 has no identity block, so the logical server has no identity of its own. stage-template.yml:88-90 signs in as the service-connection principal and runs CREATE USER [$(appName)] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER. When an Entra application or service principal runs that statement, Azure SQL resolves the name in Microsoft Graph using the server's identity, not the caller's. The server therefore needs three things. (1) An identity: system-assigned, or user-assigned set as the primary identity. (2) Graph read rights for that identity: the application permissions User.Read.All, GroupMember.Read.All and Application.Read.All with admin consent, or the Entra Directory Readers role, either assigned directly or through a role-assignable group that contains it. (3) A privileged tenant admin to grant those rights once. Granting Graph application permissions or assigning Directory Readers needs Privileged Role Administrator or Global Administrator. Microsoft's pattern is to assign Directory Readers to a role-assignable group, so that the group's owners can add each server's identity later without holding that role. What Bicep can express: identity type SystemAssigned (or UserAssigned plus primaryUserAssignedIdentityId) on the server. It is plain ARM and checkable by bicep build. What Bicep cannot express: the Graph permission grants and the Directory Readers role assignment, because these are tenant (Entra) objects, not ARM resources. The Microsoft Graph Bicep extension can declare app-role assignments. But it needs a registry restore, so gate.bicep would no longer be an offline build, and the deploying principal would itself need AppRoleAssignment.ReadWrite.All with admin consent, a privilege the per-environment service connection should not hold. To my knowledge the extension cannot assign a Directory Readers role. So under this route the prerequisite has to be a named, documented one-off admin step outside the pipeline. A second route avoids Graph entirely: create the database user from a SID with TYPE = E instead of FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER. I understand the SID is derived from the identity's client (application) id, not its principalId; verify this against the CREATE USER docs before relying on it. Bicep exposes only principalId and tenantId for a system-assigned identity, but it does expose clientId for a user-assigned identity, so this route points to a user-assigned identity on the app. The checker can't see any of this: check-pipeline.py:79-80 only asserts Entra-only with no password. Fix direction: pick one route and make it explicit. Either give the server an identity and name the tenant-admin prerequisite (who grants it, and which permissions or role) where the grant step is defined. Or create the user by SID from an identity whose client id Bicep exposes. David must be able to say which route, and why. The runtime failure itself can only be reproduced against a live tenant, but the declaration can be asserted offline.","fingerprint":"F5-MI-SQL|infrastructure/modules/sql.bicep|no-server-identity-for-graph-lookup","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given sql.bicep and stage-template.yml, When check-pipeline.py runs, Then it fails unless one of two things holds: (a) the server declares an identity (SystemAssigned or a primary UserAssigned) and the grant step's text names the Directory Readers / Graph prerequisite; or (b) the grant step creates the user WITH SID ... TYPE = E and contains no FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER. Mutation proof: a copy of sql.bicep with the identity block removed (route a) fails, naming the check; bicep build still validates the identity block's shape (check-pipeline.py)","untestable_reason":null},
+{"id":"I2","rule_id":"F5-acceptance: idempotent migrations","layer":"persistence","files":["infrastructure/pipelines/azure-pipelines.yml",".githooks/pre-push","Directory.Packages.props"],"root_cause":"Source: R2. The same cause as S4 (I7), with the same fingerprint, so one fix closes both. Symptom: the Build stage installs dotnet-ef globally with no version (infrastructure/pipelines/azure-pipelines.yml:38), so the tool that generates the prod migration script floats with whatever the hosted agent pulls on the day. Cause: the repo has no tool manifest (.config/dotnet-tools.json does not exist). The builder first wrote 'dotnet tool restore', checked for .config and a tools.json, found neither (docs/trace/build/F5/activity.jsonl:44-46), and then sed-replaced it with an unpinned global install (activity.jsonl:47). So the pipeline line is a workaround for the missing manifest, not the cause. The same gap makes pre-push's 'dotnet tool restore' (.githooks/pre-push:29) a no-op, which is the F0b carry-over (docs/qa/build-F0b.md:64, 74): machines run a global 9.0.9 against EF Core 10.0.13 (Directory.Packages.props:12). Fix direction: pin dotnet-ef to the EF Core Design version (10.0.13) in a repo tool manifest, and have the pipeline restore from it instead of installing globally. That closes the pipeline and the pre-push carry-over with one version source. Scope note for the orchestrator: the manifest path is outside infrastructure/**, so gate.scope's allowed paths must admit it with David's agreement. It is not a harness-locked path. The in-scope alternative, an inline --version on line 38, pins the pipeline only, leaves the local and pre-push carry-over open, and duplicates the version.","fingerprint":"F5-MIGRATIONS|infrastructure/pipelines/azure-pipelines.yml|dotnet-ef-unpinned-no-tool-manifest","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given azure-pipelines.yml and Directory.Packages.props, When check-pipeline.py runs, Then it fails if the Build stage contains 'tool install' without a version or a 'dotnet tool restore' with no manifest, and fails if the dotnet-ef version it resolves (from the manifest or --version) differs from the Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design PackageVersion. Mutation proof: a copy with the version removed fails (check-pipeline.py)","untestable_reason":null},
+{"id":"I3","rule_id":"code-quality: dead code","layer":"api","files":["infrastructure/modules/appservice.bicep","infrastructure/main.bicep","infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml"],"root_cause":"Source: R3. Symptom: output principalId at infrastructure/modules/appservice.bicep:51 is never read. Cause: main.bicep consumes only app.outputs.appName and app.outputs.hostName (infrastructure/main.bicep:85-86) and re-exports nothing else from the app module, and the grant step finds the identity by display name (stage-template.yml:90 uses $(appName)). The output was written in anticipation of an identity grant that the design never wired up. Dependency on I1: if the I1 fix creates the user by SID, an identity output (a client id from a user-assigned identity) gains a real consumer. Fix direction: settle I1 first. Then either delete the output, or make main.bicep and the pipeline actually consume the identity output that the chosen route needs. Bicep's linter has no rule for unused module outputs, as far as I know, so bicep build will not catch this.","fingerprint":"F5-QUALITY|infrastructure/modules/appservice.bicep|unused-principalId-output","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given the modules under infrastructure/modules, When check-pipeline.py runs, Then it fails for any module output that main.bicep never references as <module>.outputs.<name>. Mutation proof: a copy of appservice.bicep with an extra unused output fails, naming it (check-pipeline.py)","untestable_reason":null},
+{"id":"I4","rule_id":"code-quality: speculative generality","layer":"persistence","files":["infrastructure/main.bicep","infrastructure/modules/sql.bicep","infrastructure/params/dev.bicepparam","infrastructure/params/test.bicepparam","infrastructure/params/prod.bicepparam","infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml"],"root_cause":"Source: R4. Symptom: tenantId is a user-supplied parameter with a zero-GUID placeholder. Cause: infrastructure/main.bicep:30-31 declares param tenantId and passes it at :66 into sql.bicep:8, where it is used only for administrators.tenantId (sql.bicep:23). The deployment already knows its tenant: tenant().tenantId or subscription().tenantId is available at subscription scope. The parameter creates a placeholder in every param file (dev/test/prod.bicepparam:10), a variable-group entry (stage-template.yml:24) and an override argument in both the what-if and create commands (stage-template.yml:44, :59). A side benefit of removing it: it shrinks the inline-override surface that security's note flagged as untested against older az CLI versions (sqlAdminGroupObjectId keeps that question open). Fix direction: derive the tenant from the deployment context inside Bicep, and remove the parameter, the three placeholders, the overrides and the comment that mentions it.","fingerprint":"F5-QUALITY|infrastructure/main.bicep|tenantId-param-vs-deployment-tenant","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given main.bicep with no tenantId parameter, When bicep build-params runs on dev, test and prod, Then each exits 0, which proves no param file still assigns tenantId (assigning an undeclared parameter is a build-params error); and When check-pipeline.py runs, Then it fails if stage-template.yml still passes tenantId= (bicep build-params + check-pipeline.py)","untestable_reason":null},
+{"id":"I5","rule_id":"API8:2023","layer":"persistence","files":["infrastructure/modules/sql.bicep","infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml"],"root_cause":"Source: S1. Symptom: any Azure-hosted caller can reach the server's port 1433. Cause: infrastructure/modules/sql.bicep:17 sets publicNetworkAccess 'Enabled', and sql.bicep:41-48 adds the AllowAzureServices 0.0.0.0 rule, chosen so that the App Service can connect without VNet integration. The pipeline also depends on this rule: the migration and grant steps run sqlcmd from a Microsoft-hosted ubuntu-latest agent (stage-template.yml:20, :77-79, :88-90), which reaches SQL only through this public, Azure-services path. Hardening (VNet integration with a private endpoint, or rules for the app's outbound IPs) would break those two steps unless the deploy job moves to a self-hosted agent inside the network. Fix direction: this is David's call. Either record it as an accepted trade-off for an evidence-only, never-deployed node, naming the hosted-agent dependency, or harden it and redesign where migrations run. Entra-only auth (sql.bicep:24) and TLS 1.2 (:16) limit the risk, as security noted.","fingerprint":"API8|infrastructure/modules/sql.bicep|public-access-allow-azure-services","testable":false,"suggested_test":null,"untestable_reason":"The fix is a recorded design decision for David (keep it as a documented trade-off, or redesign the network and where migrations run), not a defect with one correct state an offline check could assert. If David chooses to harden, check-pipeline.py can then assert that the 0.0.0.0 rule is absent and publicNetworkAccess is Disabled."},
+{"id":"I6","rule_id":"API8:2023","layer":"api","files":["infrastructure/modules/appservice.bicep"],"root_cause":"Source: S2. Symptom: basic-auth publishing credentials for SCM/Kudu and FTP are still enabled. Cause: the Microsoft.Web/sites resource at infrastructure/modules/appservice.bicep:18-47 declares no basicPublishingCredentialsPolicies child resources (scm and ftp). ftpsState 'Disabled' at :30 turns off the FTP/FTPS protocol, but it doesn't remove the publishing-credential policy, and it doesn't touch SCM basic auth at all. Nothing needs basic auth: the pipeline deploys with az webapp deploy under the Entra-authenticated service connection (stage-template.yml:99-103). Fix direction: declare both publishing-credential policies on the site with basic auth disallowed.","fingerprint":"API8|infrastructure/modules/appservice.bicep|basic-publishing-credentials-enabled","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given appservice.bicep, When check-pipeline.py runs, Then it fails unless the site declares basicPublishingCredentialsPolicies for both scm and ftp with allow false; bicep build validates the resource type and shape. Mutation proof: a copy without the scm policy fails, naming it (check-pipeline.py + bicep build)","untestable_reason":null},
+{"id":"I7","rule_id":"NET","layer":"persistence","files":["infrastructure/pipelines/azure-pipelines.yml"],"root_cause":"Source: S4. A duplicate of R2 (I2): it is the same line (infrastructure/pipelines/azure-pipelines.yml:38), with the same root cause (no repo tool manifest, so the builder fell back to an unpinned global install, activity.jsonl:44-47) and the same fingerprint. Security frames it as supply-chain reproducibility on a floating ubuntu-latest agent; the reviewer frames it as version drift from EF Core 10.0.13. The I2 fix closes both. It is listed separately so that each source finding id stays traceable, not as a second piece of work.","fingerprint":"F5-MIGRATIONS|infrastructure/pipelines/azure-pipelines.yml|dotnet-ef-unpinned-no-tool-manifest","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Same check as I2: Given azure-pipelines.yml, When check-pipeline.py runs, Then an unversioned dotnet-ef install, or a dotnet-ef version that differs from the Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design PackageVersion, fails (check-pipeline.py)","untestable_reason":null},
+{"id":"I8","rule_id":"API8:2023","layer":"persistence","files":["infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml","infrastructure/modules/sql.bicep"],"root_cause":"Source: S3. Symptom: in every environment, including prod, the deployment service principal runs migrations and user grants as a standing SQL admin. Cause: one identity has two jobs. The service connection that deploys ARM is also a member of the Entra group that sql.bicep:18-25 makes server administrator (stated at stage-template.yml:68), and it runs the migration script (:77-79) and the grant (:88-90) with admin rights. Neither the template nor any design note records this as a deliberate trade-off. Interaction with I1: whichever route I1 takes, the caller of the grant still needs ALTER ANY USER, so creating the first user needs admin-level rights at least once. That is why a split, such as a second service connection or a narrower database user created out of band by an admin, costs more than it saves for an evidence node. Fix direction: record the standing-admin pipeline identity as an accepted trade-off where the steps are defined, and say what a split would take. Change the design only if David chooses to.","fingerprint":"API8|infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml|deploy-sp-standing-sql-admin","testable":false,"suggested_test":null,"untestable_reason":"The requested fix is a recorded design decision (accept, or split the pipeline's SQL identity). An offline check could only match the wording of a comment, which proves nothing about privilege. Who is actually in the admin group lives in Entra and the variable group, outside the repo, and az is never run."}
