+{"id":"I1","rule_id":"F5 AC2 (QA O1: approve prod after reading its what-if)","layer":"api","files":["infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml","infrastructure/pipelines/azure-pipelines.yml","docs/qa/build-F5.md","docs/trace/build/F5/loop.jsonl"],"root_cause":"Source: R1 (P1). Shares its cause with I2, listed separately so each source id stays traceable. Layer note: the schema has no infrastructure layer. As in A1, deployment and pipeline items use the nearest layer, api. SYMPTOM: the prod approver still approves before any prod what-if output exists. CAUSE, infrastructure: stage-template.yml:15-17 declares one stage, Deploy_<env>. It holds both the WhatIf job (:22-43) and the deployment job Deploy (:45), which consumes environment policies-<env> (:56). Azure DevOps evaluates checks per stage, not per job: before a stage starts, every check on every protected resource the stage uses must pass. The policies-prod approval is therefore asked when Deploy_prod is queued, before WhatIf runs, and Deploy's dependsOn WhatIf (:47) only orders the jobs after that. The comment at :19-21 ('so it runs before any approval is asked for') is false under that model. CAUSE, upstream (orchestrator): the defect was framed at job level when it was handed on. The QA tester located the problem at stage start and named the fix: docs/qa/build-F5.md:126-131 ('checks are evaluated when the stage starts' ... 'a what-if-only stage before the prod deployment stage'). The orchestrator's gate.qa receipt restates it as 'what-if runs inside the deployment job that the policies-prod approval gates' (docs/trace/build/F5/loop.jsonl:52), which drops the stage. Neither builder could have recovered O1's wording themselves. The red builder made its first Edit with no prior Read or Grep (docs/trace/build/F5/activity.jsonl:432), and so did the green builder (:439-444). Both commit subjects say 'in its own job' (:434, :452). The orchestrator records that its brief 'specified two jobs in one stage' (loop.jsonl:65), but that is its own claim. The dispatch prompts are not in the trace, so the brief's exact text can't be cited. HYPOTHESIS TEST: (a) 'approvals are evaluated per job' is not supported: nothing in the repo or receipts shows job-level evaluation, and the QA tester (O1) and the reviewer (R1) both independently cite stage-level evaluation. I can't verify it against a live Azure DevOps either. (b) 'the builder deviated from the brief' is not shown: the builders made no reads and their commit subjects match the job-level framing. (c) 'the brief asked for a separate stage' is contradicted by loop.jsonl:52 and the commit subjects. Recurrence: no A1 fingerprint matches. Within attempt 2, though, c2af74d fixed a symptom (the what-if sat inside the gated job), not the cause (it sits inside the gated stage). This fingerprint is new, and a recurrence at attempt 3 should escalate on it. FIX DIRECTION: have the template emit two stages per environment. The first is a what-if stage, WhatIf_<env>: dependsOn the incoming parameters.dependsOn, a regular job only, no environment, no deployment job, running the what-if from the drop. The second is Deploy_<env>: dependsOn WhatIf_<env>, no stage condition, and it alone holds the deployment job on environment policies-<env>. azure-pipelines.yml keeps its dependsOn values (Build, Deploy_dev, Deploy_test), because those feed the what-if stage. The prod sequence becomes Deploy_test → WhatIf_prod, which runs unapproved → Deploy_prod, which asks the approval when it is queued, after WhatIf_prod has succeeded. The approval check must live on the policies-<env> environment only. If an approval were put on a resource the what-if stage also uses (its ARM service connection or the policies-<env> variable group), that stage would pause first and the defect would come back. Say this where the environment comment is (stage-template.yml:55, azure-pipelines.yml:3). Fix the false comment at :19-21.","fingerprint":"F5-AC2-QA-O1|infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml|whatif-inside-approval-gated-stage","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given stage-template.yml, When check-pipeline.py runs, Then it passes only if four things hold: (1) the stage that runs az deployment sub what-if declares no environment on any job and contains no deployment job; (2) the stage holding the deployment job on environment policies-<env> lists the what-if stage's name in its stage-level dependsOn; (3) that deploying stage has no condition; (4) no what-if runs in any stage that declares an environment. Mutation proof: a copy of the c2af74d layout (WhatIf job inside Deploy_<env>) fails, and so does a copy whose deploy stage has condition: always(). The runtime approval timing itself can't be asserted offline, only the structure that produces it (check-pipeline.py)","untestable_reason":null},
+{"id":"I2","rule_id":"anti-oracle: QA what-if checker assertions","layer":"tests","files":["infrastructure/pipelines/check-pipeline.py","infrastructure/pipelines/pipeline.schema.json","docs/trace/build/F5/loop.jsonl"],"root_cause":"Source: R2 (P1). Same cause as I1: the job-level model of approvals, carried from the gate.qa framing (loop.jsonl:52) into the red assertions. Listed separately, not merged. SYMPTOM: the checker passes on the layout R1 fails. CAUSE: check-pipeline.py:62 reads only template['stages'][0]['jobs'], and :76-86 assert separation at job level. They check for a regular what-if job, no environment on it, no what-if in the deployment job, and dependsOn from the deployment job. None of them asserts which stage consumes the environment, or that the what-if runs in a different stage that the deploying stage depends on. The comment at :74-75 claims approval timing that the checks don't prove. :85-86 only repeat :76, :72 and :84. The mutation proof made at green (loop.jsonl:54, mutant without dependsOn) tested the same job-level property, so no gate could catch the defect: red, green and mutation all encoded the brief's model. RESTRUCTURE HAZARD: three more places hard-code stages[0]. These are :62-63, the deploy lookup, where next() with no default would raise StopIteration as a traceback instead of a named failure if the what-if stage comes first; :153, the I5 sql_jobs; and :181, A2/R2 all_steps. After the split, each would read the wrong stage or crash. All earlier assertions (I1-I8, A2, A2.2) must keep inspecting the deployment job and every job. Also, pipeline.schema.json:43 allows a stage-level condition, which could run Deploy_<env> after a failed what-if stage. FIX DIRECTION: assert the property at stage level, as in I1's test. Find stages and jobs by key or name across all template stages, never by index. Reject a condition on the deploying stage, in the checker or by removing condition from the stage schema. Drop the redundant :85-86 check, or make it assert the stage-level order. Make the mutation proof the c2af74d layout. Routed to the builder, because the tester can't write infrastructure/**.","fingerprint":"F5-AC2-QA-O1|infrastructure/pipelines/check-pipeline.py|checker-asserts-job-not-stage-separation","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given a scratch copy of infrastructure/ with stage-template.yml at the c2af74d layout (WhatIf job and Deploy deployment job in one stage, Deploy_<env>), When check-pipeline.py runs on it, Then it exits 1, naming the stage-separation check. Given the fixed two-stage template, Then it exits 0, and the I5, I8 and A2/R2 checks still find the deployment job's SQL and AzureCLI@2 steps (check-pipeline.py, mutation run by the orchestrator at gate.finding-red and gate.green)","untestable_reason":null},
+{"id":"I3","rule_id":"QA script currency","layer":"tests","files":["docs/qa/build-F5.md","docs/trace/build/F5/loop.jsonl"],"root_cause":"Source: R3 (P2). SYMPTOM: the QA script points David at lines that no longer exist. Step 17 (docs/qa/build-F5.md:80) cites stage-template.yml 41-53/55-75 as one job. Step 10 (:66) cites az at 48/63/128, but HEAD has 38/76/141. Step 18 (:81) cites the deployment job at 19 and the environment at 28-29, but HEAD has 45 and 55-56. Steps 27, 32 and 33 (:98, :103, :104) cite 21-27, 28-29 and 83-90, which have also moved. O1 (:126-131) still says create follows the what-if 'in the same job', and the run header (:108) is at b92de26. CAUSE, orchestrator process: gate.qa closed the QA defect in-task, with no attempt bump (loop.jsonl:52). The sequence after it, red 6fe2b8d → green c2af74d → re-review (loop.jsonl:53-64, activity.jsonl:432-465), has no step that re-runs or re-dates the QA script against the fixed commit. So every in-task QA fix leaves the script describing the pre-fix layout. This is not a tester error: the script was correct at b92de26. Recurrence: none in A1. The F4 QA step 34 in-task closure cited at loop.jsonl:52 used the same path. FIX DIRECTION: after the I1/I2 fix lands, re-run the QA script against the new head. Re-cite every stage-template.yml line, rewrite step 17, step 18 and O1 for the two-stage layout, and record the new commit under test. Sequence it after I1, because I1 moves the lines again.","fingerprint":"F5-QA|docs/qa/build-F5.md|stale-line-refs-after-in-task-fix","testable":false,"suggested_test":null,"untestable_reason":"The defect is prose line citations in a manual QA script. They are confirmed by the tester's re-run against the fixed head, and no automated test in the repo parses docs/qa line references. A checker that matched cited numbers to file contents would test the document's wording, not the pipeline's behaviour."},
+{"id":"I4","rule_id":"API8:2023","layer":"api","files":["infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml","infrastructure/pipelines/azure-pipelines.yml","infrastructure/pipelines/check-pipeline.py"],"root_cause":"Source: security S1 (P2, non-blocking). SYMPTOM: the unapproved what-if exercises prod ARM access. CAUSE: the WhatIf job's AzureCLI@2 at stage-template.yml:34 uses parameters.azureServiceConnection. That is the same connection that runs az deployment sub create (:72) and az webapp deploy (:137), and azure-pipelines.yml:77 binds it to sc-policies-prod, a connection with subscription-scope write rights. The what-if needs read plus the deployment what-if action only. INTERACTION WITH I1: the I1 restructure makes the unapproved what-if a deliberate design property, not an accident of job order, so this question has to be settled as part of it. S1's alternative, an approval check on the service connection, conflicts with I1. Checks are evaluated for every protected resource a stage uses, so an approval on the shared ARM connection would gate the what-if stage too and reproduce the QA defect. Non-approval checks that evaluate automatically, such as branch control, don't conflict. ANSWER: yes, the restructured what-if stage should get its own lower-privilege ARM connection per environment, passed as a separate template parameter. It is the only S1 remedy that keeps the approval after the what-if. Two caveats. (1) I understand Reader alone isn't enough for what-if, because it needs Microsoft.Resources/deployments/whatIf/action at subscription scope, and possibly the validate action, beyond */read. A custom role (Reader plus those actions) is likely needed. Verify this against the ARM what-if permissions doc before writing it down. (2) This adds three out-of-repo prerequisites: a connection and role assignment per environment. Those belong with the other prerequisites in QA section C, and in the README carry-over to F6. It's David's call, because S1 is P2: he may inste